  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_23",
      "text": "WORKFLOW: crepuscular (bimodal) activity. Many species are crepuscular, active around dawn and dusk rather than in one consolidated bout. Two things follow. First, the dominant peak in the periodogram is at 12 h, a harmonic of the two daily bouts, with only a small peak at 24 h (there because dawn and dusk are 13 h apart here, not exactly 12). A strong 12 h component is often bimodality rather than a separate rhythm. Second, and more dangerous, a single circular mean phase is actively misleading here: with two peaks roughly 12 h apart the resultant vector is short and its direction lands BETWEEN them, at the time of day the animal is least active. The Rayleigh node shows this: time of day weighted by activity gives a low R and an acrophase in the night, and the clock plot shows the two peaks that one number hides. If the two peaks were exactly 12 h apart they would cancel completely, R would be close to 0, and the mean phase would be set by noise. Always look at the distribution before you summarise it with one number.",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 24,
      "width": 360,
      "height": 190
    }
